WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.088
Advanced grouping creates subtotal rows where
grouped columns can appear as NULL

00:00:04.975 --> 00:00:08.250
The challenge is that real source data can also
contain genuine NULL

00:00:08.275 --> 00:00:12.300
values If we simply use COALESCE and replace every
NULL with a

00:00:12.325 --> 00:00:15.912
label such as All products we can accidentally
confuse a real missing

00:00:15.938 --> 00:00:21.475
value with a subtotal placeholder GROUPING solves
that problem It tells

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.138
us whether a column is absent because PostgreSQL
rolled that dimension up

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:30.025
for a subtotal With two grouping expressions the
grouping code can represent

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:35.038
several cases Code zero means both category and
product participate in the

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:40.550
grouping Other codes indicate that one expression
has been rolled up Code

00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:44.938
three represents the grand total where both
dimensions are rolled up You

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:49.800
do not need to memorize every numeric code
immediately The important idea

00:00:49.812 --> 00:00:54.075
is that GROUPING gives you metadata about the
summary level Whenever a

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.212
subtotal report can contain real NULL values use
GROUPING rather than guessing

00:00:58.238 --> 00:01:02.850
based on NULL alone That makes the report
semantically correct and prevents

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:07.588
misleading labels This is a good example of how
analytical SQL is

00:01:07.612 --> 00:01:12.112
not only about calculation It is also about making
sure users can

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.162
correctly interpret what each row means
